Q3. Two-way communication between parent and child using pipe
============================================================

Question:
Write a program to handle the two-way communication between parent
and child using pipe.

#include<stdio.h>
#include<unistd.h>
#include<string.h>
#include<sys/wait.h>

int main()
{
    int p1[2],p2[2];
    char msg[50];

    pipe(p1);
    pipe(p2);

    if(fork()==0)
    {
        close(p1[1]);
        close(p2[0]);

        read(p1[0],msg,sizeof(msg));
        printf("Child received: %s\n",msg);

        write(p2[1],"Hello Parent",13);
    }
    else
    {
        close(p1[0]);
        close(p2[1]);

        write(p1[1],"Hello Child",12);

        read(p2[0],msg,sizeof(msg));
        printf("Parent received: %s\n",msg);

        wait(NULL);
    }

    return 0;
}

OUTPUT:
Child received: Hello Child
Parent received: Hello Parent

1-LINE EXPLANATIONS:
pipe()        - Creates a pipe for communication between processes.
fork()        - Creates the child process so parent and child can communicate.
read()        - Reads data from the read end of a pipe.
write()       - Writes data to the write end of a pipe.
close()       - Closes an unused pipe end.
wait()        - Makes the parent wait for the child to finish.
p1            - First pipe used for Parent -> Child communication.
p2            - Second pipe used for Child -> Parent communication.
p[0]          - Read end of a pipe.
p[1]          - Write end of a pipe.

WHY TWO PIPES?
One pipe provides one-way communication, so two pipes are used here
to achieve two-way communication as required by the question.

============================================================
SYSTEM CALLS / FUNCTIONS GIVEN IN THE PDF
============================================================

fork()        - Creates a new process (child process).
getpid()      - Returns the process ID of the calling process.
atexit()      - Registers a function to execute at normal termination.
getenv()      - Fetches the value of a specified environment variable.
putenv()      - Adds or changes an environment variable using name=value.
setenv()      - Sets an environment variable to a specified value.
unsetenv()    - Removes an environment variable.
getrlimit()   - Gets the resource limits of a process.
wait()        - Suspends the calling process until a child terminates.
exec()        - Replaces the current process image with a new program.
execlp()      - Executes a program and searches for it using PATH.
pipe()        - Creates a communication channel between processes.
exit()        - Terminates a process.

============================================================
PDF TOPICS TO REMEMBER
============================================================

Process:
A process is a program in execution.

Process sections:
Stack - Contains temporary data such as function parameters, return addresses and local variables.
Heap  - Contains dynamically allocated memory.
Text  - Contains the program instructions.
Data  - Contains global and static variables.

fork() return values:
0        - Returned to the child.
Positive - Child PID returned to the parent.
Negative - Child creation failed.

exec():
The exec family replaces the current process image with a new program.
The PID does not change because exec() does not create a new process.

wait():
The wait() system call suspends the calling process until one of its
children terminates.
